    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Vinnie Pasquantino** (76.21) anchoring the lineup, standing out as our clear-cut top play against Keider Montero. **Vinnie Pasquantino** benefits from a high-leverage spot in the order and a favorable matchup against a pitcher with a 4.45 xERA, making him an elite start. **Josh Naylor** (67.01) and **James Wood** (61.94) round out our tier-one options; the former exploits the matchup against Sean Burke, while **James Wood** receives a significant boost from his leadoff spot, effectively insulating his floor despite a neutral platoon situation against Robby Snelling. Meanwhile, **Tyler Soderstrom** (61.14) benefits from a 16% park factor adjustment, keeping him in the green despite a high-skill opponent in Kyle Bradish.\n\nWe are navigating several complex platoon dynamics, specifically with **Brice Turang** and **Zach McKinstry**. Both face left-handed pitching today, and while our optimizer acknowledges the efficiency loss, they remain in the lineup due to the scarcity of stolen bases in our 5x5 format. The most difficult bench decision involved **Mickey Moniak** (43.66). While he received a wind-aided boost and carries significant speed upside, the combination of facing a high-skill arm like Jes\u00fas Luzardo and the high probability of being erased on the basepaths by an elite catcher pushed him just outside our starting nine. We continue to prioritize consistent plate appearances over high-variance speed plays when the opposing battery is stifling.\n\nCaution is advised across the board today as all lineup spots remain \"Pending.\" **Corbin Carroll** (59.90) and **Gabriel Moreno** (42.12) are specifically tethered to the projected order; should **Corbin Carroll** drop out of the three-hole, his valuation will see a sharp contraction. Additionally, **Austin Riley** (45.36) is flirting with our \"marginal\" threshold, as his exposure to the high-skill Emmet Sheehan leaves little room for error. We are keeping **Jose Caballero**, **Kyle Karros**, and **Travis Bazzana** on standby, but given the current risk profiles, we will only deploy them if we see a confirmed shift in batting order that significantly increases their projected run-scoring opportunity.",
